<!-- This is the source of the vignette. Edit this file, never vignettes/ausparl.Rmd, and
rebuild with `make vignette` (needs AUSPARL_DB). The generated file is precomputed against the
full database, so building it needs neither the database nor the network. -->

ausparl queries the Australian Hansard database: every sitting of the House of
Representatives and the Senate since 1901, split into turns (speeches, questions, answers and
interjections) and linked to members, parties, ministries and electorates. This vignette walks
through a typical analysis in eight steps. It was computed on the full database; the code works
the same on the small sample database bundled with the package, though the answers differ.
For a shorter worked example to run from start to finish, see the `example/` folder of the
[GitHub repository](https://github.com/Australian-Parliamentary-Speech/ausparl).

```{r packages, message = FALSE}
library(ausparl)
library(dplyr)
library(ggplot2)
```

## 1. Getting the data

The database is a single DuckDB file, `hansard.duckdb`, of about 9.5 GB. ausparl does not
ship it. Each ausparl release is pinned to one version of the database on Zenodo, which
`db_download()` fetches:

```{r download, eval = FALSE}
db_download()
```

It saves the database in your user data directory, resumes an interrupted download when you
run it again, and checks the file before installing it. After that, `db_connect()` and every
other ausparl function use it with no further setup. To keep the file in a folder of your
own instead, give it with `db_download(dir = "dataset")`, which returns the installed file's
path, and open that copy with `db_connect(path = )`.

If you already have a copy, point the `AUSPARL_DB` environment variable at it instead, for
example in your `~/.Renviron`. It takes precedence over the downloaded copy:

```{r env-var, eval = FALSE}
Sys.setenv(AUSPARL_DB = "/path/to/hansard.duckdb")
```

Every ausparl function then connects to it when first needed. To try the package without the
full database, connect to the sample bundled with it instead:

```{r sample, eval = FALSE}
db_connect(sample = TRUE)
```

The database is always opened read-only, and DuckDB's memory use is capped by the
`ausparl.memory_limit` option (3 GB by default).

## 2. Connect and check the version

`db_connect()` with no arguments opens the database named by `AUSPARL_DB`. `db_info()`
reports which build you are using; quote its `schema_version` and `built_at` in your methods.

```{r connect}
db_connect()
db_info() |>
  select(schema_version, built_at, n_sittings, n_actions, n_members, fts_available)
```

## 3. Find a member

Members are identified by their Parliamentary Handbook id, `PHID`. `find_member()` looks
one up from pieces of a name, in any order and any case.

```{r find-member}
howard <- find_member("john howard")
howard |> select(PHID, Gender, State, chambers, first_service, last_service)
```

`member_profile()` gathers everything recorded about one member into a list of tables.
A ministerial role reads as `Role`, `Prep` and `Entity` together, such as "Minister Assisting
the Prime Minister".

```{r profile}
profile <- member_profile("ZD4")
sapply(profile, nrow)
profile$parl_service |> select(Electorate, DateStart, DateEnd) |> head(3)
profile$ministry_records |>
  distinct(Role, Prep, Entity, RDateStart, RDateEnd) |>
  arrange(RDateStart)
```

## 4. That member's turns

`get_speeches()` returns one row per turn. Here we take John Howard's turns as a Liberal
in the 41st Parliament (2004-2007), his last as Prime Minister. The `party` filter applies to
the party at the time of the turn, so it also works for members who changed party.
`include_text = FALSE` leaves out the speech text, which keeps the result small.

```{r howard-41}
turns <- get_speeches(
  member = "ZD4",
  party = "Liberal Party of Australia",
  parliament_id = 41,
  include_text = FALSE
)
nrow(turns)
turns |> count(is_speech, is_question, is_answer)
```

With `collect = FALSE` the result stays in the database as a lazy table, so dplyr verbs run
inside DuckDB. Here we count his turns in every parliament he sat in.

```{r howard-all}
get_speeches(member = "ZD4", include_text = FALSE, collect = FALSE) |>
  count(parliament_id) |>
  arrange(parliament_id) |>
  collect()
```

## 5. Search for a term

`search_speeches_by_terms()` finds the turns that use a word or phrase. It returns one row
per matching turn, with `n_hits`, the exact number of occurrences in that turn. Counts by year
or by speaker are ordinary aggregations over that result. Use `collect = FALSE` so that they run
inside DuckDB, and `include_text = FALSE` so the text is not loaded.

```{r search}
hits <- search_speeches_by_terms(
  "immigration",
  from = 1990, to = 2020,
  include_text = FALSE, collect = FALSE
)

hits |>
  summarise(n_turns = n(), n_occurrences = sum(n_hits, na.rm = TRUE)) |>
  collect()
```

Mentions per year:

```{r search-by-year}
by_year <- hits |>
  group_by(year = year(date)) |>
  summarise(n_turns = n(), n_occurrences = sum(n_hits, na.rm = TRUE)) |>
  collect() |>
  arrange(year)
head(by_year)
```

```{r search-by-year-plot, fig.cap = "Occurrences of 'immigration' per year, 1990-2020.", fig.alt = "Line chart of the number of times 'immigration' is said in Parliament each year from 1990 to 2020."}
ggplot(by_year, aes(year, n_occurrences)) +
  geom_line() +
  geom_point() +
  labs(x = NULL, y = "Occurrences", title = "\"immigration\" in Hansard, 1990-2020")
```

Who mentioned it most? Joining to the members table adds names. We keep only turns with a
member id (`PHID`).

```{r search-by-speaker}
hits |>
  filter(!is.na(PHID)) |>
  group_by(PHID) |>
  summarise(n_turns = n(), n_occurrences = sum(n_hits, na.rm = TRUE)) |>
  left_join(get_members(columns = c("PHID", "DisplayName"), collect = FALSE), by = "PHID") |>
  arrange(desc(n_occurrences)) |>
  head(10) |>
  collect()
```

## 6. Add context

The `add_` functions add what was true of the speaker on the day of each turn. Here we take
the 2005 turns that mention immigration and add each speaker's party, role and whether their
party was in government. `add_party()` notes that the search result already carries the
party columns and adds only the abbreviation.

```{r context}
turns_2005 <- search_speeches_by_terms("immigration", from = 2005, to = 2005,
                                       include_text = FALSE)
with_context <- turns_2005 |>
  add_party() |>
  add_role() |>
  add_government_status()

with_context |>
  select(date, PHID, party_abbrev_at_time, role_at_time, government_status_at_time) |>
  head()

with_context |> count(government_status_at_time, role_at_time)
```

Turns with no member id get `NA` in the added columns.

## 7. Speaking activity

`member_activity()` counts turns, speeches, questions, answers, interjections and words for
each member, parliament and chamber. Here we compare members of the 46th Parliament
(2019-2022) by gender, taking `Gender` from the members table.

```{r activity}
activity <- member_activity(parliament_id = 46) |>
  left_join(get_members(columns = c("PHID", "Gender")), by = "PHID")

activity |>
  group_by(chamber, Gender) |>
  summarise(
    members = n(),
    median_turns = median(n_turns),
    median_words = median(n_words),
    .groups = "drop"
  )
```

```{r activity-plot, fig.cap = "Words spoken per member in the 46th Parliament, by gender.", fig.alt = "Box plots of words spoken per member in the 46th Parliament, by gender, for the House and the Senate."}
ggplot(activity, aes(Gender, n_words)) +
  geom_boxplot() +
  facet_wrap(~chamber) +
  scale_y_log10() +
  labs(x = NULL, y = "Words spoken (log scale)", title = "Words per member, 46th Parliament")
```

## 8. Hand off to text analysis

`as_corpus()` turns a result with text into a quanteda corpus, keeping the other columns as
document variables. Here we take twenty of John Howard's turns that mention immigration while
he was Prime Minister.

```{r corpus}
howard_immigration <- search_speeches_by_terms(
  "immigration",
  member = "ZD4", chamber = "house", from = 1996, to = 2007,
  limit = 20
)
corp <- as_corpus(howard_immigration)
corp
```

```{r disconnect}
db_disconnect()
```
